    2015     6 |       38      283    13.4% |        8       50    16.0% |     63% (24 of 38)
    2016     5 |       22      643     3.4% |       15      109    13.8% |     14% (3 of 22)
    2017     4 |       37      674     5.5% |       19      124    15.3% |     14% (5 of 37)
    2018     3 |       69      745     9.3% |       23      122    18.9% |     16% (11 of 69)
    2019     2 |      146      840    17.4% |       34      132    25.8% |     23% (33 of 146)
    2020     1 |       55      565     9.7% |       23       98    23.5% |     15% (8 of 55)
    2021     0 |        0       19     0.0% |        0        1     0.0% |      0% (0 of 0)

  NOTEBOOK level (the authors' unit)       trend z =  -4.40  p = 1.06e-05    OR/year = 0.882
  REPOSITORY level (the independent unit)  trend z =  -1.87  p = 0.0618      OR/year = 0.906

  If the notebook-level trend is much stronger than the repository-level
  one, the age association is about which REPOSITORIES are old, not about
  what happens to code as it ages.

==========================================================================
Q3 -- THE SAME PIPELINE, TWO YEARS APART, ON OVERLAPPING COHORTS
==========================================================================
    year |           2021 run |           2023 run | rate ratio
    2013 |     6/129   =   4.7% |    69/141   =  48.9% | x10.5
    2014 |    16/217   =   7.4% |    27/317   =   8.5% | x1.2
    2015 |    38/283   =  13.4% |    60/235   =  25.5% | x1.9
    2016 |    22/643   =   3.4% |    51/420   =  12.1% | x3.5
    2017 |    37/674   =   5.5% |    75/666   =  11.3% | x2.1
    2018 |    69/745   =   9.3% |   208/1283  =  16.2% | x1.8
    2019 |   146/840   =  17.4% |   267/1549  =  17.2% | x1.0
    2020 |    55/565   =   9.7% |   231/3670  =   6.3% | x0.6
    2021 |     0/19    =   0.0% |   121/1368  =   8.8% | xinf

  Same code, same variable (GitHub repository creation year), same team,
  overlapping repositories. If a cohort's rate can move by an order of
  magnitude between two runs of one pipeline, the cohort rate is not a
  property of the cohort. Neither run's age trend should be believed:
  the 2021 data give a NEGATIVE age association and the 2023 table a
  strongly POSITIVE one (age_trend.py: z = +11.7).

